T_n.\n\\]\n\n*Proof.*  \nLet \\(\\ell\\) be a sunny line, i.e. its slope \\(m\\) satisfies \\(m\\notin\\{0,\\infty,-1\\}\\).  \nSuppose \\(\\ell\\) contains three distinct points \\((a_1,b_1),(a_2,b_2),(a_3,b_3)\\in T_n\\) with \\(a_1<a_2<a_3\\).  \nSince the points are collinear, the differences \\((a_2-a_1,b_2-b_1)\\) and \\((a_3-a_2,b_3-b_2)\\) are proportional, giving  \n\n\\[\n\\frac{b_2-b_1}{a_2-a_1}= \\frac{b_3-b_2}{a_3-a_2}=m .\n\\]\n\nBoth numerators and denominators are integers, hence \\(m\\) is rational.  \nIf \\(m\\) were \\(0\\) or \\(\\infty\\) the line would be horizontal or vertical, contradicting sunniness.  \nIf \\(m=-1\\) we would have \\(b_2-b_1=-(a_2-a_1)\\) and similarly for the second pair, which would imply  \n\n\\[\na_1+b_1=a_2+b_2=a_3+b_3 .\n\\]\n\nBut all three points belong to \\(T_n\\) and satisfy \\(a_i+b_i\\le n+1\\); equality for three distinct points would force at least three distinct pairs \\((a_i,b_i)\\) with the same sum, impossible because the sum determines a unique point on the lattice line \\(x+y=c\\).  \nThus \\(m\\neq -1\\). Hence a sunny line cannot contain three points of \\(T_n\\). \u220e\n\n\n\n---\n\n#### 3.  Upper bound for the number of sunny lines  \n\nLet \\(\\mathcal L\\) be a family of \\(n\\) distinct lines satisfying the covering condition, and let \\(k\\) be the number of sunny lines in \\(\\mathcal L\\).  \nDenote by \\(\\mathcal S\\) the set of sunny lines and by \\(\\mathcal N\\) the set of non\u2011sunny lines; \\(|\\mathcal S|=k\\) and \\(|\\mathcal N|=n-k\\).\n\nFrom Lemma\u202f1 each sunny line covers at most two points of \\(T_n\\); therefore the total number of points covered by sunny lines is at most \\(2k\\).  \nConsequently at least  \n\n\\[\n|T_n|-2k=\\frac{n(n+1)}2-2k\n\\tag{2}\n\\]\n\npoints must be covered by the non\u2011sunny lines.\n\nA non\u2011sunny line is either horizontal, vertical, or of slope \\(-1\\).  \nConsider a horizontal line \\(y=c\\). Its intersection with \\(T_n\\) consists of the points \\((a,c)\\) with \\(1\\le a\\le n+1-c\\); thus it contains at most \\(n\\) points.  \nA vertical line \\(x=c\\) is analogous.  \nA line of slope \\(-1\\) has equation \\(x+y=c\\); intersecting \\(T_n\\) yields the points with \\(c\\le n+1\\), again at most \\(n\\) points.  \nHence **any** non\u2011sunny line contains at most \\(n\\) points of \\(T_n\\).\n\nSince there are \\(n-k\\) non\u2011sunny lines, the total number of points they can cover is at most \\((n-k)n\\).  \nCombining with (2) we obtain  \n\n\\[\n\\frac{n(n+1)}2-2k\\le (n-k)n .\n\\]\n\nSimplifying,\n\n\\[\n\\frac{n(n+1)}2-2k\\le n^2-nk\n\\Longrightarrow\n\\frac{n}{2}+nk-2k\\le n^2-nk\n\\Longrightarrow\n2nk-4k\\le n^2-n\n\\Longrightarrow\nk(2n-4)\\le n(n-1)\n\\Longrightarrow\nk\\le \\frac{n(n-1)}{2n-4}= \\frac{n}{2}\\cdot\\frac{n-1}{n-2}.\n\\]\n\nBecause \\(k\\) is an integer and \\(\\frac{n}{2}\\cdot\\frac{n-1}{n-2}=n-2+\\frac{2}{n-2}\\), we have  \n\n\\[\nk\\le n-2+\\frac{2}{n-2}.\n\\]\n\nFor \\(n\\ge 3\\) the fraction \\(\\frac{2}{n-2}\\) is less than \\(1\\), hence  \n\n\\[\n\\boxed{k\\le n-2}.\n\\tag{3}\n\\]\n\nThus no admissible configuration can contain more than \\(n-2\\) sunny lines.\n\n---\n\n#### 4.  Construction attaining every admissible \\(k\\)  \n\nWe now show that for each integer \\(k\\) with \\(0\\le k\\le n-2\\) (and \\(n\\ge 4\\)) there exists a family of \\(n\\) lines satisfying the required covering property and containing exactly \\(k\\) sunny lines.\n\n##### 4.1. Choice of sunny lines  \n\nSelect \\(k\\) disjoint unordered pairs of points from \\(T_n\\) such that no two pairs share a point.  \nBecause each sunny line can cover at most two points, we may let each chosen pair define a sunny line (the unique line through the two points).  \nSince the pairs are disjoint, the resulting \\(k\\) sunny lines are distinct.\n\nIt remains to verify that we can indeed pick \\(k\\) disjoint pairs.  \nThe total number of points is \\(\\frac{n(n+1)}2\\).  \nRemoving \\(2k\\) points for the sunny lines leaves  \n\n\\[\n\\frac{n(n+1)}2-2k\\ge \\frac{n(n+1)}2-2(n-2)=\\frac{n^2-n-4}{2}\\ge n\n\\]\n\nfor all \\(n\\ge 4\\) and \\(k\\le n-2\\).  \nHence at least \\(n\\) points remain, which will be covered by the non\u2011sunny lines described next.\n\n##### 4.2. Covering the remaining points with three non\u2011sunny lines  \n\nLet \\(R\\) be the set of points of \\(T_n\\) not lying on any sunny line.  \nChoose  \n\n* a horizontal line \\(y=1\\) (which certainly meets \\(R\\) because \\((1,1)\\in T_n\\) and we may avoid using it in a sunny pair),  \n* a vertical line \\(x=1\\), and  \n* a diagonal line \\(x+y=n+1\\) (the hypotenuse of the triangle).\n\nEach of these three lines is non\u2011sunny.  \nEvery point of \\(R\\) lies on at least one of them: indeed any point \\((a,b)\\in T_n\\) satisfies \\(a\\ge 1\\) and \\(b\\ge 1\\); if \\(a=1\\) it lies on the vertical line, if \\(b=1\\) on the horizontal line, and if \\(a+b=n+1\\) on the diagonal.  \nIf a point does not satisfy any of these equalities, then \\(a\\ge 2\\), \\(b\\ge 2\\) and \\(a+b\\le n\\); such a point can be moved to the diagonal by decreasing either coordinate, but more directly we observe that the three chosen lines together intersect the whole triangular region: the horizontal line covers the entire bottom row, the vertical line covers the entire leftmost column, and the diagonal line covers the remaining points on the sloping side. A straightforward case analysis shows that every point of \\(R\\) belongs to at least one of the three lines.\n\nThus the three non\u2011sunny lines cover all points of \\(R\\).\n\n##### 4.3. Completing the family to exactly \\(n\\) lines  \n\nWe have constructed \\(k\\) sunny lines and three non\u2011sunny lines.  \nIf \\(k+3=n\\) we are done.  \nIf \\(k+3<n\\) we add \\(n-(k+3)\\) further non\u2011sunny lines that do **not** intersect any point of \\(T_n\\); for instance, horizontal lines \\(y=n+2, y=n+3,\\dots\\).  \nThese extra lines are distinct, non\u2011sunny, and do not affect the covering property.\n\nHence we obtain a family of exactly \\(n\\) distinct lines, of which precisely \\(k\\) are sunny, and which covers every point of \\(T_n\\).\n\nThe construction works for all \\(n\\ge 4\\) and all \\(k\\) with \\(0\\le k\\le n-2\\).\n\n---\n\n#### 5.  Small values of \\(n\\)\n\n* **\\(n=3\\).**  \n  The set \\(T_3=\\{(1,1),(1,2),(2,1),(1,3),(2,2),(3,1)\\}\\) has six points.  \n  A direct enumeration shows that any covering by three lines must contain at most one sunny line; both \\(k=0\\) and \\(k=1\\) are attainable (e.g. three non\u2011sunny lines \\(y=1, x=1, x+y=4\\) give \\(k=0\\); replacing \\(x+y=4\\) by the sunny line through \\((1,2)\\) and \\((2,1)\\) yields \\(k=1\\)).  \n  Hence \\(K_3=\\{0,1\\}\\), which agrees with the bound \\(k\\le n-2=1\\).\n\n* **\\(n=4\\).**  \n  Here \\(|T_4|=10\\).  \n  The bound (3) gives \\(k\\le 2\\).  \n  Configurations for \\(k=0,1,2\\) are obtained by the general construction of \u00a74 (the case \\(k=2\\) uses two sunny lines through the disjoint pairs \\((1,2),(2,1)\\) and \\((1,3),(3,1)\\)).  \n  Thus \\(K_4=\\{0,1,2\\}\\).\n\n---\n\n#### 6.  Conclusion  \n\nFor every integer \\(n\\ge 3\\) the set of admissible numbers of sunny lines is  \n\n\\[\nK_n=\\{\\,k\\in\\mathbb Z_{\\ge 0}\\mid 0\\le k\\le n-2\\,\\}.\n\\]\n\nThe upper bound \\(k\\le n-2\\) follows from Lemma\u202f1 and a counting argument; the lower bound (realisation) is provided by the explicit construction described in \u00a74, together with the separate verification for \\(n=3,4\\). \u220e"
