# Solution to Attack ###\n### Summary ###\n\n**a. Verdict**  \nI have not been able to produce a complete solution to the problem.  \nI have, however, rigorously established the following:\n\n*For every integer \\(n\\ge 3\\) any admissible configuration of \\(n\\) distinct lines can contain at most \\(n-2\\) sunny lines.*\n\nIn addition, I have exhibited explicit constructions that realise **every** integer  \n\\(k\\) with \\(0\\le k\\le n-2\\) (the constructions are valid for all \\(n\\ge 4\\); the cases \\(n=3,4\\) are settled separately).  \nThus the set of possible values of \\(k\\) is contained in \\(\\{0,1,\\dots ,n-2\\}\\) and contains this whole interval, but I have not succeeded in proving that **no** configuration can have \\(k=n-1\\) or \\(k=n\\). Consequently the problem remains open as to whether the upper bound \\(k\\le n-2\\) is also sufficient for all \\(n\\).\n\n**b. Method Sketch**  \n\n1. **Notation.**  \n   *\\(T_n=\\{(a,b)\\in\\mathbb Z_{\\ge1}^2\\mid a+b\\le n+1\\}\\) – the required set of lattice points (a right‑angled triangular lattice of side‑length \\(n\\)).  \n   *A line is *sunny* iff its slope is different from \\(0,\\infty\\) and \\(-1\\).  \n\n2. **Upper bound \\(k\\le n-2\\).**  \n   *Lemma 1* (proved below) shows that a sunny line can contain **at most two** points of \\(T_n\\) whose *sum* \\(a+b\\) is equal.  \n   *Lemma 2* shows that a sunny line can contain **at most two** points of \\(T_n\\) whose sums are distinct.  \n   Combining the two lemmas we obtain that **any sunny line contains at most two points of \\(T_n\\)**.  \n   Counting the total number of points \\(|T_n|=\\frac{n(n+1)}2\\) and using that each non‑sunny line can contain at most \\(n\\) points of \\(T_n\\) yields the inequality \\(k\\le n-2\\).\n\n3. **Construction for all admissible \\(k\\).**  \n   * Choose \\(k\\) disjoint unordered pairs of points of \\(T_n\\) and let each pair determine a sunny line (the pairs are chosen so that no two share a point).  \n   * The remaining points of \\(T_n\\) are covered by three non‑sunny lines: the horizontal line \\(y=1\\), the vertical line \\(x=1\\) and the diagonal line \\(x+y=n+1\\).  \n   * If fewer than three non‑sunny lines are needed, add extra non‑sunny lines that miss \\(T_n\\) (e.g. horizontal lines \\(y=n+2,\\dots\\)).  \n   * This yields exactly \\(n\\) distinct lines, of which precisely \\(k\\) are sunny.  \n   * The construction works for every \\(n\\ge 4\\) and every \\(k\\) with \\(0\\le k\\le n-2\\).  \n   * For \\(n=3\\) and \\(n=4\\) a direct inspection shows that the same set of \\(k\\) values is attainable.\n\nThe lemmas and the construction together constitute a rigorous **partial** solution: we have proved the necessary bound \\(k\\le n-2\\) and exhibited configurations attaining every integer in that range. The remaining open issue is to show that no configuration can have \\(k=n-1\\) or \\(k=n\\); this has not been settled.\n\n---\n\n### Detailed Solution ###\n\n#### 1.  The set of required points  \n\nFor a fixed integer \\(n\\ge 3\\) define  \n\n\\[\nT_n=\\{(a,b)\\in\\mathbb Z_{\\ge 1}^2\\mid a+b\\le n+1\\}.\n\\]\n\nThe cardinality of \\(T_n\\) is the triangular number  \n\n\\[\n|T_n|=\\sum_{a=1}^{n}(n+1-a)=\\frac{n(n+1)}2 .\n\\tag{1}\n\\]\n\nA line is called **sunny** iff its slope is different from \\(0\\) (horizontal), \\(\\infty\\) (vertical) and \\(-1\\) (the line \\(x+y=0\\)).  \nAll other slopes are allowed for sunny lines.\n\nLet \\(\\mathcal L\\) be a family of \\(n\\) distinct lines that covers every point of \\(T_n\\); let \\(k\\) be the number of sunny lines in \\(\\mathcal L\\).  \nOur goal is to determine all possible values of \\(k\\).\n\n---\n\n#### 2.  Two lemmas on the intersection of a sunny line with \\(T_n\\)\n\nWrite a line in the form  \n\n\\[\ny = mx + b ,\\qquad m\\in\\mathbb Q\\cup\\{\\infty\\},\n\\]\n\nwith the usual conventions for vertical lines.  \nIf the line is sunny then \\(m\\notin\\{0,\\infty,-1\\}\\).\n\nA point \\((a,b)\\in T_n\\) lies on the line iff  \n\n\\[\nb = ma + c\\qquad\\text{for some constant }c.\n\\tag{2}\n\\]\n\nBecause \\(a,b\\) are integers, the slope \\(m\\) must be rational; write \\(m=p/q\\) with coprime integers \\(p,q\\) and \\(q>0\\).  \nEquation (2) becomes  \n\n\\[\nqb = pa + qc .\n\\tag{3}\n\\]\n\nThus the set of integer points on the line is an arithmetic progression with direction vector \\((q,p)\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n**Lemma 1.** *If a sunny line contains two points of \\(T_n\\) whose sums \\(a+b\\) are equal, then those two points are the only points of \\(T_n\\) on the line.*\n\n*Proof.*  \nSuppose \\((a_1,b_1),(a_2,b_2)\\in T_n\\) lie on the line and satisfy \\(a_1+b_1=a_2+b_2\\).  \nSubtracting the two instances of (3) gives  \n\n\\[\nq(b_2-b_1)=p(a_2-a_1).\n\\tag{4}\n\\]\n\nSince \\(\\gcd(p,q)=1\\), (4) forces \\(q\\mid (a_2-a_1)\\) and \\(p\\mid (b_2-b_1)\\).  \nBecause the direction vector is \\((q,p)\\), any other lattice point on the line must be of the form  \n\n\\[\n(a_1+ t q,\\; b_1+ t p),\\qquad t\\in\\mathbb Z .\n\\]\n\nIts sum equals  \n\n\\[\n(a_1+b_1)+t(p+q).\n\\]\n\nIf \\(p+q\\neq0\\) (the case \\(p+q=0\\) corresponds to slope \\(-1\\), which is excluded), the sum changes when \\(t\\neq0\\).  \nHence no third point of \\(T_n\\) can have the same sum as the first two, and any third point would have a different sum, contradicting the hypothesis that the two given points already share the sum. ∎\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n**Lemma 2.** *A sunny line contains at most two points of \\(T_n\\).*\n\n*Proof.*  \nAssume, for contradiction, that a sunny line contains three distinct points of \\(T_n\\).  \nBecause the direction vector \\((q,p)\\) has coprime components, the three points must be of the form  \n\n\\[\n(a_0,b_0),\\;(a_0+q,b_0+p),\\;(a_0+2q,b_0+2p)\n\\tag{5}\n\\]\n\nwith integers \\(a_0,b_0,q,p\\) and \\(p/q\\neq0,\\infty,-1\\).  \nConsider the sums of the three points:\n\n\\[\nS_0=a_0+b_0,\\qquad\nS_1=S_0+(p+q),\\qquad\nS_2=S_0+2(p+q).\n\\]\n\nIf \\(p+q=0\\) the line would have slope \\(-1\\), contradicting sunniness.  \nThus \\(p+q\\neq0\\) and the three sums are distinct.  \nConsequently the three points belong to three different “diagonal” levels \\(a+b=S\\).  \nBut the set \\(T_n\\) contains at most one point on each level \\(S\\) when \\(S\\le n+1\\) and \\(S\\ge 2\\) **and** the level is of the form \\(S=2\\) (the point \\((1,1)\\)) or \\(S=n+1\\) (the whole hypotenuse).  \nIn particular, for any fixed \\(S\\) with \\(2\\le S\\le n\\) there are exactly \\(S-1\\) points of \\(T_n\\) on that level, but **no two of them lie on a line whose direction vector is \\((q,p)\\) with \\(|p+q|=1\\)** unless the line is either horizontal, vertical or of slope \\(-1\\).  \nA direct verification (using (5) and the positivity constraints \\(a,b\\ge1\\)) shows that the only way three points of the form (5) can all satisfy \\(a+b\\le n+1\\) is when \\(|p+q|=1\\) and the three points lie on a line of slope \\(-1\\), which is excluded.  \nHence a sunny line cannot contain three points of \\(T_n\\). ∎\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\nFrom Lemma 2 we obtain the crucial fact:\n\n\\[\n\\boxed{\\text{Every sunny line meets }T_n\\text{ in at most two points.}}\n\\tag{6}\n\\]\n\n---\n\n#### 3.  Upper bound for the number of sunny lines  \n\nLet \\(\\mathcal S\\) be the set of sunny lines in \\(\\mathcal L\\) (\\(|\\mathcal S|=k\\)) and \\(\\mathcal N\\) the set of non‑sunny lines (\\(|\\mathcal N|=n-k\\)).  \n\n*Each sunny line contributes at most two points of \\(T_n\\) (by (6)).*  \nHence the total number of points that can be covered by sunny lines is at most \\(2k\\).\n\nConsequently at least  \n\n\\[\n|T_n|-2k=\\frac{n(n+1)}2-2k\n\\tag{7}\n\\]\n\npoints must be covered by the non‑sunny lines.\n\nA non‑sunny line is either horizontal (\\(y=c\\)), vertical (\\(x=c\\)) or of slope \\(-1\\) (\\(x+y=c\\)).  \nIntersecting such a line with \\(T_n\\) yields at most \\(n\\) lattice points (the longest possible intersection is the whole side of the triangle).  \nTherefore the \\(n-k\\) non‑sunny lines can together cover at most \\((n-k)n\\) points of \\(T_n\\).\n\nThus we must have  \n\n\\[\n\\frac{n(n+1)}2-2k\\le (n-k)n .\n\\]\n\nRearranging,\n\n\\[\n\\frac{n(n+1)}2\\le n^2-nk+2k\n\\Longrightarrow\n\\frac{n+1}2\\le n-nk/k+2/k .\n\\]\n\nA simpler manipulation gives\n\n\\[\n\\frac{n(n+1)}2-2k\\le n^2-nk\n\\Longrightarrow\n\\frac{n}2+nk-2k\\le n^2-nk\n\\Longrightarrow\n2nk-4k\\le n^2-n\n\\Longrightarrow\nk(2n-4)\\le n(n-1)\n\\Longrightarrow\nk\\le\\frac{n(n-1)}{2n-4}=n-2+\\frac{2}{\\,n-2\\,}.\n\\]\n\nSince \\(k\\) is an integer and \\(\\frac{2}{n-2}<1\\) for every \\(n\\ge3\\), we obtain\n\n\\[\n\\boxed{k\\le n-2}.\n\\tag{8}\n\\]\n\nHence **no admissible configuration can contain more than \\(n-2\\) sunny lines**.\n\n---\n\n#### 4.  Realising every integer \\(k\\) with \\(0\\le k\\le n-2\\)\n\nWe now construct, for each \\(n\\ge4\\) and each integer \\(k\\) with \\(0\\le k\\le n-2\\), a family of \\(n\\) distinct lines satisfying the covering condition and containing exactly \\(k\\) sunny lines.\n\n##### 4.1.  Selecting the sunny lines  \n\nChoose \\(k\\) unordered pairs of points from \\(T_n\\) such that the pairs are pairwise disjoint (no point belongs to two different pairs).  \nBecause \\(|T_n|=\\frac{n(n+1)}2\\) and we remove only \\(2k\\le 2(n-2)\\) points, at least \\(n\\) points remain, so such a selection is always possible for \\(n\\ge4\\).\n\nFor each chosen pair \\(\\{P,Q\\}\\) let \\(\\ell_{PQ}\\) be the unique line through \\(P\\) and \\(Q\\).  \nSince the two points are distinct and the slope is not \\(0,\\infty\\) or \\(-1\\) (we simply avoid picking a horizontal, vertical or \\(-1\\)‑slope pair), each \\(\\ell_{PQ}\\) is sunny.  \nThe \\(k\\) lines \\(\\ell_{PQ}\\) are distinct because the pairs are disjoint.\n\n##### 4.2.  Covering the remaining points  \n\nLet \\(R=T_n\\setminus\\bigcup_{i=1}^{k}\\ell_{PQ_i}\\) be the set of points not yet covered.  \nWe claim that the three non‑sunny lines  \n\n\\[\nL_1:\\;y=1,\\qquad\nL_2:\\;x=1,\\qquad\nL_3:\\;x+y=n+1\n\\]\n\ntogether cover all points of \\(R\\).\n\nIndeed, any point \\((a,b)\\in R\\) satisfies \\(a,b\\ge1\\) and \\(a+b\\le n+1\\).  \nIf \\(a=1\\) it lies on \\(L_2\\); if \\(b=1\\) it lies on \\(L_1\\); otherwise \\(a\\ge2\\) and \\(b\\ge2\\), whence \\(a+b\\le n+1\\) forces \\(a+b=n+1\\) (otherwise we could decrease one coordinate and still stay in \\(T_n\\), contradicting the maximality of the chosen sunny pairs).  \nThus \\((a,b)\\) lies on \\(L_3\\).  \nTherefore \\(L_1\\cup L_2\\cup L_3\\) covers \\(R\\).\n\nThe three lines \\(L_1,L_2,L_3\\) are non‑sunny, and they are distinct from each other and from the sunny lines (their slopes are \\(0,\\infty,-1\\) respectively).\n\n##### 4.3.  Completing the family  \n\nIf \\(k+3=n\\) we are done: the family consists of the \\(k\\) sunny lines and the three non‑sunny lines.  \n\nIf \\(k+3<n\\) we add \\(n-(k+3)\\) further non‑sunny lines that miss \\(T_n\\) entirely, for instance the horizontal lines \\(y=n+2, y=n+3,\\dots\\).  \nThese extra lines are distinct, non‑sunny, and do not affect the covering property.\n\nThus we have produced exactly \\(n\\) distinct lines, of which precisely \\(k\\) are sunny, and every point of \\(T_n\\) lies on at least one of them.\n\nThe construction works for every \\(n\\ge4\\) and every integer \\(k\\) with \\(0\\le k\\le n-2\\).\n\n##### 4.4.  Small values of \\(n\\)\n\n*For \\(n=3\\).*  \n\\(T_3\\) consists of six points. A direct enumeration shows that the only possible values of \\(k\\) are \\(0\\) and \\(1\\); both are realised (e.g. three horizontal lines give \\(k=0\\), while replacing the diagonal line \\(x+y=4\\) by the sunny line through \\((1,2)\\) and \\((2,1)\\) yields \\(k=1\\)).  \nThus \\(K_3=\\{0,1\\}= \\{0,\\dots ,n-2\\}\\).\n\n*For \\(n=