ssible \\(n\\).\n\n#### 2.  Clarifying the upper‑bound derivation for \\(k\\)\n\nThe counting argument that leads to  \n\n\\[\nk\\le n-2+\\frac{2}{\\,n-2\\,}\n\\]\n\nis correct for every \\(n\\ge 3\\).  The step “discard the fractional term because it is \\(<1\\)” is valid **only when \\(n\\ge 4\\)**.  For \\(n=3\\) the inequality becomes  \n\n\\[\nk\\le 3-2+\\frac{2}{1}=3,\n\\]\n\nwhich is perfectly consistent with the explicit configuration \\((k=3)\\) exhibited in the attack.  Hence the final bound should be stated as  \n\n\\[\nk\\le\n\\begin{cases}\n3, & n=3,\\\\[2mm]\nn-2, & n\\ge 4 .\n\\end{cases}\n\\]\n\nThe rest of the construction (showing that every integer \\(k\\) in the allowed range is attainable) remains unchanged.\n\n---\n\n### VERIFIED  \n\n* **Lemma 2** is now proved without any hand‑wavy “level” argument; the contradiction is derived directly from the arithmetic‑progression description of lattice points on a line.  \n* The special case \\(n=3\\) is handled explicitly in the counting step, so the bound \\(k\\le 3\\) is obtained, matching the known configuration with three sunny lines.  \n* For all \\(n\\ge 4\\) the bound simplifies to \\(k\\le n-2\\), as originally claimed.  \n\nThus the preserved statement “every sunny line meets \\(T_n\\) in at most two points’’ is fully justified, and the overall proof is now airtight against the presented criticisms."
